The `poly` function takes a list of coefficients `xs` and a point `x`, and evaluates the polynomial with those coefficients at that point. It does this by iterating over the list of coefficients, multiplying each one by `x` raised to the power of its index (using the `enumerate` function), and summing all of these values.

The `find_zero` function finds a root of the polynomial using the bisection method. It starts by finding two points `begin` and `end` such that the polynomial has different signs at these points. It then repeatedly bisects the interval between `begin` and `end` and adjusts `begin` or `end` to have the same sign as the polynomial at the new point. This process continues until the interval is small enough (less than 1e-10 in this case). The function then returns the midpoint of the final interval as the root of the polynomial.

